Microsoft Windows NT V3.5

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Windows NT V3.5 supports the new AlphaStation and AlphaServer systems
   announced in this issue, in addition to already shipping systems

o  Improved performance and many new features

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Windows NT V3.5 (the beta product name was "Daytona") is now available and
   supports the new AlphaStation and AlphaServer systems. These include:

o  AlphaStation 200 4/166, 4/233, and 400 4/233 systems.
o  AlphaServer 1000 4/200, 2000 4/200, 2100 4/200 Rackmount, and 2100 4/200
   CAB systems.

V3.5 also supports the already shipping Alpha products: the DECpc AXP 150,
the DEC 2000 models 300 and 500 and AlphaServer 2100 4/200 system,
previously named the Digital 2100 Server.

New product names more clearly differentiate capabilities of the two
versions of this Microsoft product:

o  Windows NT Workstation V3.5 -- provides the power of a workstation with
   the ease of use, productivity, and compatibility of a PC.

o  Windows NT Server V3.5 -- the network foundation for running the new
   generation of business applications.

This version of Windows NT improves overall system performance. V3.5 shows a
4 to 8 Mbyte reduction in memory requirements -- this release requires less
memory to run efficiently. The underlying 286 Intel emulation for 16-bit
applications for Windows and MS-DOS operating systems has been greatly
improved for RISC systems. This has resulted in major improvements in the
performance of these applications.

Windows NT V3.5 now supports multiple MS-DOS based machines (VDMs). The
result of this is that 16-bit applications can now be multitasked with each
application inhabiting its own process space. Because of the improvements in
memory usage that have been added for V3.5 on server systems (with
comparable configurations), there has been a 20 percent improvement in
TCP/IP over V3.1 of Windows NT Server.

With this new version, users can connect to NetWare Servers out of the box.
NetWare Workstation Compatible Software (NWCS) allows Windows NT clients
access to NetWare files, print, and application servers. This release
supports the next generation of Object-Oriented applications, and native
OLE2 is supported with full 16-bit to 32-bit application integration.

Symmetric Multiprocessing (SMP) is supported for the Alpha AXP platforms
with the availability of the Digital AlphaServer family running Windows NT
Server. Industry standard OpenGL support for graphics applications has also
been added. New and faster TCP/IP and IPX/SPX stacks with automatic
addressing and configuration is included. Windows NT Server includes all of
the new features in Windows NT Workstation plus additional new features,
such as account lockout and system dump facilities for easy troubleshooting
and easy dial-in to NetWare and UNIX Servers.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

With the final release of "Daytona," Digital will discontinue shipments of
Windows NT V3.1 products on all platforms (Intel and Alpha AXP) and
implement Windows NT V3.5 products under the same part numbers.

Users who have purchased a Digital 2100 Server system (now called the
AlphaServer 2100 system) with Windows NT beta software will be automatically
updated to the production release at no additional charge.

Update Program

o  Users who have purchased Windows NT or Windows NT Advanced Server V3.1
   between May 15, 1994 and production availability of V3.5 will receive an
   update to V3.5 at no charge (shipping and handling may be applied).

o  Users who have purchased Windows NT or Windows NT Advanced Server V3.1
   before May 15, 1994 will be charged for Windows NT Workstation and for
   Windows NT Server (includes 250 client access licenses).

o  Users who wish to upgrade from Windows NT to Windows NT Server will be
   charged (includes 20 client access licenses).

Users will need to provide the DEC number of their order to confirm the
order date.

New Licensing

With Windows NT V3.5, Microsoft has modified its server software licensing
policy. A client access license is now required by each client using a
Windows NT Server. A client may access any number of Windows NT Servers in
