ZLX-L1 3D Solids Midrange Graphics Accelerator

HIGHLIGHTS

o  3D midrange graphics leadership

o  Best price for combination of image quality and speed

o  Scalability with the host CPU

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

The ZLX-L1 graphics accelerator is a 24 bit, Z buffered TURBOchannel
graphics card. It offers exceptional midrange 3D leadership at a competitive
price. It places the geometry processing back at the Alpha host CPU,
allowing the graphics performance to scale up with the processor. It is
designed to take advantage of the speeds availed by the DEC 3000 Models 300,
700, and 900 and is capable of rendering 1.4 million 3D vectors per second
and demonstrating PLBsrf (picture level benchmarking for surfaces) of up to
100. It occupies two TURBOchannel slots.

Digital offers a range of graphics products on DEC 3000 workstations to suit
the vast majority of graphics users in MCAD, CAMD, GIS, entertainment, and
medical imaging. These users need high-speed 2D, high-speed 3D wireframe, or
midrange-performance 3D solids. Digital also supports this hardware with the
most popular graphics Application Programming Interfaces (APIs).

The ZLX-E series provides the fastest 2D performance available at any price.
ZLX-E is included with the DEC 3000 Models 600, 700, and 900. In addition,
all ZLX-E graphics options support entry-performance 3D solids graphics, for
those users who work with shaded solid models part of the time.

Users who work with shaded solid models most of the time will benefit from
the ZLX-L1 and the ZLX-M2 graphics accelerators. These are midrange
performance 3D solids accelerators. The ZLX-L1 yields the industry's best
price for true color 3D graphics with solids performance of over 100
PLBsurf93. The ZLX-L1 and ZLX-M2 offer similar performance. The ZLX-M2 is
recommended if the user or application requires large amounts of additional
graphics memory for holding off-screen images or for OpenGL accumulation
buffering or stencil buffering. The ZLX-L1 leverages the power of the Alpha
host CPU to perform much of the 3D graphics processing. Hence, while
graphics is being drawn, the CPU contributes to that and does no other work
until the graphics are displayed. The ZLX-M2 fully off-loads the host
workstation from all graphics computations. If the application is structured
so that application specific processing on the CPU occurs simultaneously
with graphics processing on the graphics board, then the ZLX-M2 is the
better choice.

Digital supports all the above graphics by the X, PEX, PHIGS, GKS, and
OpenGL APIs. These are the most important industry standards for graphics
programming. PHIGS and GKS are available via layered products DEC PHIGS and
DEC GKS. PEX and OpenGL are available via the DEC Open3D product. OpenGL is
emerging as the dominant 3D standard. Digital is commited to optimizing all
of its graphics options for Open3D. Digital is also a charter member of the
OpenGL architecture board, the Application Review Board.

