system-related PC LAN management strengths of PATHWORKS ManageWORKS with the
strengths of PC LAN system management applications. Preferred Systems'
AuditWare software now contains a built in interface to ManageWORKS. System
managers will now be able to manage the network operating system (NOS) in
their LANs, and, generate reports about the NetWare servers in their LANs --
from a single console.

AuditWare is a Windows-based reporting tool developed by Preferred Systems,
Inc. for use in Novell NetWare environments. It is the first available tool
that provides true multi-server support. The software contains a Windows-
based custom report writer for exception reporting. Its extensible
architecture allows external databases to be linked into any report.

As a first step in the relationship between Digital and Preferred Systems,
existing AuditWare software has been modified to include an interface to
ManageWORKS. ManageWORKS is the management framework tool for Digital's
PATHWORKS software. It is a component of PATHWORKS for DOS and Windows V5.

Using ManageWORKS, the PC system manager can from a single console:

o  Manage PATHWORKS servers
o  Manage retail LAN Manager (V2.X)
o  Manage NetWare (V2.X, V3.X) servers
o  Remotely configure PATHWORKS clients
o  Simultaneously view and manage multiple servers and clients

Using AuditWare, the PC LAN system manager can generate reports about:

o  File servers
o  Disk volumes
o  The operating system
o  The NOS
o  Workgroup managers
o  Security equivalencies
o  Groups
o  Users
o  Trustees
o  Workstation restrictions
o  Print queues, server printers, printer definitions, and print jobs
o  Dangerous users
o  Passwords

BENEFITS

Users are downsizing large solutions to client/server systems, using PCs for
mission-critical applications and trying to manage the chaos and expense
this activity is causing in their PC LAN environments.

AuditWare will help users better understand and control some of their PC LAN
costs because:

o  It captures server configurations into a database and then runs the
   reports.
o  It allows system managers to get a representation of how things are today
   and compare the results to changes over time.
o  Its multi-server views allow system managers to separate their
   environments into whatever views make sense, and look at them in
   different ways.
o  System mangers can use more than 30 canned reports and create their own
   custom reports.
o  It is extensible to existing data if the existing information can be
   written in an xBase format.

