Coexistence Tools for OpenVMS Platform Packaged with DEC OSF/1

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Limited time no-charge offer for six key tools for coexistence between
   OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 operating systems

o  Tools supplied and supported by Boston Business Computing and Sector 7,
   and distributed by Digital

o  Tools packaged with the DEC OSF/1 media H-kit until August 1, 1995

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

On September 9, 1994, Digital Equipment Corporation and leading
interoperability software vendors Boston Business Computing, Ltd. and Sector
7, USA Inc. announced a limited time no-charge offer for six key tools for
coexistence between OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 platforms.

The coexistence tools, developed, supplied, and supported by Boston Business
Computing and Sector 7, are being distributed by Digital. The tools are
packaged with the DEC OSF/1 media H-kit until August 1, 1995. There will be
no charge for the coexistence tools license, media, or support.

After August 1, 1995, the free offer and distribution will end and the
coexistence tools will be sold directly from Boston Business Computing and
Sector 7.

The OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 Interoperability Tools CD-ROM (AV-QBX6A-XE)
contains software from Boston Business Computing, Ltd. and OpenVMS emulation
software from Sector 7 that will run on DEC OSF/1 systems. These free
programs and support will be available only until August 1, 1995 and can be
installed on DEC OSF/1 V3.0 (or later) systems with any number of users.

Boston Business Computing Ltd. provides EDT, Backup, DCL, and Mail
functionality with its EDT+, VBackup, VCL, and Vmail products for DEC OSF/1
platforms. Sector 7 provides DCL and print and job spooler functionality
with its VX/DCL and VX/JSP products for DEC OSF/1 systems.

>From Boston Business Computing, Ltd.

o  EDT+ -- EDT+ 6.0, a full screen text editor, implements all the features
   of VAX EDT on UNIX. EDT+ gives you over 150 commands and qualifiers,
   making it one of the most powerful editors available today. Because EDT+
   lets users move easily between OpenVMS platforms and UNIX, users benefit
   immediately from the elimination of the training time, costs, and
   frustration of learning a new editor. Additional features define screen
   color, control the location of editor files with environment variables,
   record and playback keystrokes with LEARN mode, and run UNIX and other
   programs from within EDT+.

o  Vbackup -- Vbackup provides full system backup capabilities. Systems can
   run in normal multi-user mode while Vbackup performs a full system
   backup. Vbackup locks files against multiple write access, and detects
   and warns of potential inconsistencies if other processes override locks.
   Vbackup saves and restores all standard and many nonstandard UNIX file
   types and characteristics, including hard and soft links, sparse files,
   file ownership and protection, access and modification times, AIX trusted
   computing base attribute, special files, and FIFOs. Vbackup translates
   files and their attributes to a unified format that OpenVMS BACKUP and
   Vbackup running on any system can read. Because of Vbackup's portable
   data format, Vbackup provides a way to transfer files and file systems
   across OpenVMS platforms and heterogeneous UNIX platforms.

o  VCL -- VCL emulates the Digital Command Language (DCL) on UNIX. VCL lets
   users continue using familiar OpenVMS commands as they move from the
   OpenVMS platform to UNIX. VCL provides over 50 commands and utilities
   with over 300 qualifiers. Users can include system commands, program
   data, logical expressions, lexicals, and interactive user prompts in VCL
   command files. VCL supports complete VMS file specifications. The
   following VMS utilities are among those provided with VCL: DIFFERENCES,
   DUMP, ENCRYPT, MERGE, REPLY, SEARCH, and SORT. VCL lexicals allow string
   manipulation and provide information about directories, files, and
   general system parameters.

o  Vmail -- Vmail is a user interface for UNIX mail that emulates Digital's
   OpenVMS Mail utility. It lets users continue using familiar VMS MAIL
   commands as they move to the UNIX environment. With Vmail, users can
   correspond transparently with UNIX mail users on the network. Vmail
   provides over 39 commands such as READ, SEND, FILE, and EXTRACT. For
   added flexibility, lists of messages can be affected with a single
   command without the need to SELECT and MOVE each message to a special
   folder. With Vmail, either OpenVMS or UNIX style names can be used when
   referencing external files. Vmail also incorporates some UNIX like mail
   functions.

>From Sector 7

o  VX/DCL -- VX/DCL is used to supply a VAX style user interface for VX/JSP.
   VX/DCL maximizes inhouse skills by supplying an OpenVMS interface to UNIX
   systems. It supports most of the common DCL verbs, over 100 lexical
   functions, both local and global symbols, and logical names. VX/DCL
   performs "on the fly" translation of filenames between OpenVMS and UNIX
   platforms and provides an interface to VX/JSP.

o  VX/JSP -- VX/JSP is a print and job spooler that provides VAX JOB and
   PRINT queue management. VX/JSP allows jobs to be submitted to either
   BATCH or PRINT queues. Full user definable queues are supported with the
   ability for each batch queue to execute multiple batch jobs
   simultaneously (/JOB_LIMIT). A full interface is provided via the DCL
   PRINT/SUBMIT/SET/DEFINE cld, or via SYS$SNDJBCW and SYS$GETQUIW. Printing
   capability has been extended to allow VAX QUEUE management and either
   direct or UNIX spool printing.

For more information on these products, please refer to the documentation
provided on the CD-ROM.

BENEFITS

o  Easiest coexistence -- The availability of these tools with the DEC OSF/1
   product are proof that coexistence with OpenVMS platforms and UNIX is
   easier with Digital's UNIX, the DEC OSF/1 operating system.

o  Ease of use -- Moving to Digital's UNIX does not require OpenVMS users to
   learn anything new. Users are implementing client/server computing
   environments on the UNIX operating system. It is important to them to
   maintain both user and application consistency with existing systems and
   software in use today. These tools allow users to see exactly the same
   interface they have been using with their current applications, and
   developers can work with familiar programming interfaces and editors.

o  Lower training costs -- Users may feel they face a challenge in training
   OpenVMS users and application developers in UNIX. The tools make editing,
   mail, command line, and backup functions identical between UNIX systems
   and OpenVMS systems, thus eliminating these obstacles. This also allows
   managers to schedule UNIX training as appropriate since users can
   immediately use DEC OSF/1 systems with OpenVMS like interfaces.

o  Cost effective transition -- Users are building more mixed environments
   today. These tools, now shipped with the DEC OSF/1 operating system at no
   charge, help users manage coexistence more easily and help maintain
   investments in hardware, software, applications, and training.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The coexistence tools packaged with the DEC OSF/1 product are available now.
The tools from Boston Business Computing and Sector 7 do not require a
license key when distributed by Digital. The products are distributed with a
paper license from Boston Business Computing and Sector 7, allowing
installation on an unlimited number of systems with an unlimited number of
users on each system running the DEC OSF/1 V3.n family of releases. The
installation procedure states that the user is licensed to use this software
with DEC OSF/1 V3.0 or later.

